<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Josiah Wedgwood</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1730">1730</a>–<a href="/years/1795">1795</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josiah">Josiah</a> Wedgwood was an English potter, entrepreneur and abolitionist who founded the Wedgwood company in 1759 and led the industrialisation of European pottery manufacture. Through systematic experiment he developed improved pottery bodies, among them creamware, black basalt and jasperware, and his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a> Works opened in 1769. A pioneer of modern marketing, he used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> showrooms, direct mail, money-back guarantees and illustrated catalogues. He was elected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a> in 1783 for a pyrometer to measure kiln temperatures, and made the anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> medallion Am I Not a Man and a Brother? He was the grandfather of Charles and Emma Darwin.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/josiah-wedgwood.jpg" alt="Josiah Wedgwood" width="330" height="433" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Daderot · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Josiah_Wedgwood_by_George_Stubbs,_1780,_enamel_on_a_Wedgwood_ceramic_tablet_-_Wedgwood_Museum_-_Barlaston,_Stoke-on-Trent,_England_-_DSC09537.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
